## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate feature namespaces for different teams and enforce RBAC?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward-compat migrations?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes multi-tenant governance, scale, streaming semantics, schema drift handling, and retention policies across a large lakehouse. It also tests trade-offs between Spark and Flink for high-volume streaming and the practicality of per-tenant partitioning and access control.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data contracts and lineage
- Exactly-once streaming and idempotent upserts
- Iceberg partitioning by tenant/region
- Schema drift handling and canonical schema
- Data retention, GDPR-style deletes, and privacy masking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate event against per-tenant contract before upsert
function process(event, tenant) {
  const contract = registry.get(tenant);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  iceberg.merge(event, canonicalKey, tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift across tenants over time?
- What metrics and alerts would you monitor for data quality and lineage?
